    content: "# Function Notation & Linear Forms\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 1 of 10 · Linear Functions & Systems · Lesson 1*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** use f(x), slope-intercept, point-slope, and standard form interchangeably.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a savings plan for college costs that might use **Function Notation & Linear Forms**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 1 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nLinear relationships are constant-rate stories. Tables, graphs, and equations must agree. Today's skill — **Function Notation & Linear Forms** — builds that habit inside **Linear Functions & Systems**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nBudgets, break-even points, and training plans are linear models before they become curves. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **slope** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **intercept** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **solution set** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a savings plan for college costs. Goal aligned to “Function Notation & Linear Forms.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 4, 1, 3.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Treating slope as rise/run with flipped coordinates, or declaring a system has no solution after one arithmetic slip.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Function Notation & Linear Forms, computed with visible steps using 4 and 1, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Function Notation & Linear Forms” but change the story to a construction slope measurement. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Treating slope as rise/run with flipped coordinates, or declaring a system has no solution after one arithmetic slip.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nLinear fluency is the launch pad for systems, inequalities, and later function comparisons.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Function Notation & Linear Forms” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 4 and 1. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a savings plan for college costs). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. A line has slope 4 and y-intercept 2. Write y = mx + b, then find y when x = 9.\n2. A line has slope 2 and y-intercept 2. Write y = mx + b, then find y when x = 9.\n3. A line has slope 6 and y-intercept 0. Write y = mx + b, then find y when x = 5.\n4. A line has slope 6 and y-intercept 3. Write y = mx + b, then find y when x = 3.\n5. A line has slope 5 and y-intercept 4. Write y = mx + b, then find y when x = 9.\n6. A line has slope 3 and y-intercept 1. Write y = mx + b, then find y when x = 6.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. y = 4x + 2; when x=9, y = 38.\n2. y = 2x + 2; when x=9, y = 20.\n3. y = 6x + 0; when x=5, y = 30.\n4. y = 6x + 3; when x=3, y = 21.\n5. y = 5x + 4; when x=9, y = 49.\n6. y = 3x + 1; when x=6, y = 19.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Function Notation & Linear Forms” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 1: Linear Functions & Systems.\n",
