    content: "# Systems by Graphing & Tables\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 1 of 10 · Linear Functions & Systems · Lesson 5*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** estimate solutions graphically and check with substitution into both equations.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a small business break-even table that might use **Systems by Graphing & Tables**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 1 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nLinear relationships are constant-rate stories. Tables, graphs, and equations must agree. Today's skill — **Systems by Graphing & Tables** — builds that habit inside **Linear Functions & Systems**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nBudgets, break-even points, and training plans are linear models before they become curves. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **slope** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **intercept** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **solution set** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a small business break-even table. Goal aligned to “Systems by Graphing & Tables.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 3, 3, 4.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Treating slope as rise/run with flipped coordinates, or declaring a system has no solution after one arithmetic slip.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Systems by Graphing & Tables, computed with visible steps using 3 and 3, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Systems by Graphing & Tables” but change the story to a science lab data set. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Treating slope as rise/run with flipped coordinates, or declaring a system has no solution after one arithmetic slip.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nLinear fluency is the launch pad for systems, inequalities, and later function comparisons.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Systems by Graphing & Tables” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 3 and 3. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a small business break-even table). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. Solve the system: x + y = 6, 4x − y = 19. Show substitution or elimination.\n2. Solve the system: x + y = 5, 3x − y = 11. Show substitution or elimination.\n3. Solve the system: x + y = 10, 2x − y = 11. Show substitution or elimination.\n4. Solve the system: x + y = 12, 4x − y = 28. Show substitution or elimination.\n5. Solve the system: x + y = 10, 3x − y = 18. Show substitution or elimination.\n6. Solve the system: x + y = 2, 2x − y = 7. Show substitution or elimination.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. x = 5, y = 1. Check: 5+1=6; 4(5)−1=19.\n2. x = 4, y = 1. Check: 4+1=5; 3(4)−1=11.\n3. x = 7, y = 3. Check: 7+3=10; 2(7)−3=11.\n4. x = 8, y = 4. Check: 8+4=12; 4(8)−4=28.\n5. x = 7, y = 3. Check: 7+3=10; 3(7)−3=18.\n6. x = 3, y = -1. Check: 3+-1=2; 2(3)−-1=7.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Systems by Graphing & Tables” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 1: Linear Functions & Systems.\n",
