    objectives: "• Solve 2×2 linear systems by substitution; verify both equations.\n• Show algebraic structure with labeled steps.\n• Check solutions (substitution, estimate, or domain).",
    content: "# Systems by Substitution\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 1 of 10 · Linear Functions & Systems · Lesson 6*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** solve 2×2 linear systems by substitution; verify both equations.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a small business break-even table that might use **Systems by Substitution**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 1 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nLinear relationships are constant-rate stories. Tables, graphs, and equations must agree. Today's skill — **Systems by Substitution** — builds that habit inside **Linear Functions & Systems**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nBudgets, break-even points, and training plans are linear models before they become curves. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **slope** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **intercept** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **solution set** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a small business break-even table. Goal aligned to “Systems by Substitution.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 11, -1, 3.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Treating slope as rise/run with flipped coordinates, or declaring a system has no solution after one arithmetic slip.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Systems by Substitution, computed with visible steps using 11 and -1, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Systems by Substitution” but change the story to a science lab data set. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Treating slope as rise/run with flipped coordinates, or declaring a system has no solution after one arithmetic slip.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nLinear fluency is the launch pad for systems, inequalities, and later function comparisons.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Systems by Substitution” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 11 and -1. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a small business break-even table). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. Solve the system: x + y = 3, 4x − y = 17. Show substitution or elimination.\n2. Solve the system: x + y = 15, 3x − y = 25. Show substitution or elimination.\n3. Solve the system: x + y = 7, 2x − y = 11. Show substitution or elimination.\n4. Solve the system: x + y = 9, 4x − y = 26. Show substitution or elimination.\n5. Solve the system: x + y = 7, 3x − y = 17. Show substitution or elimination.\n6. Solve the system: x + y = 5, 2x − y = 1. Show substitution or elimination.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. x = 4, y = -1. Check: 4+-1=3; 4(4)−-1=17.\n2. x = 10, y = 5. Check: 10+5=15; 3(10)−5=25.\n3. x = 6, y = 1. Check: 6+1=7; 2(6)−1=11.\n4. x = 7, y = 2. Check: 7+2=9; 4(7)−2=26.\n5. x = 6, y = 1. Check: 6+1=7; 3(6)−1=17.\n6. x = 2, y = 3. Check: 2+3=5; 2(2)−3=1.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Systems by Substitution” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 1: Linear Functions & Systems.\n",
