    objectives: "• Solve quadratic inequalities using roots and sign charts.\n• Show algebraic structure with labeled steps.\n• Check solutions (substitution, estimate, or domain).",
    content: "# Quadratic Inequalities\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 2 of 10 · Quadratic Functions · Lesson 7*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** solve quadratic inequalities using roots and sign charts.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a savings plan for college costs that might use **Quadratic Inequalities**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 2 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nQuadratics encode acceleration and max/min. Forms trade which features are visible. Today's skill — **Quadratic Inequalities** — builds that habit inside **Quadratic Functions**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nProjectile height, profit near a peak, and arched designs all need vertex and roots. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **vertex** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **discriminant** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **axis of symmetry** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a savings plan for college costs. Goal aligned to “Quadratic Inequalities.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 9, -3, 3.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Forgetting ± when taking square roots, or stopping at the quadratic formula without checking.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Quadratic Inequalities, computed with visible steps using 9 and -3, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Quadratic Inequalities” but change the story to a construction slope measurement. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Forgetting ± when taking square roots, or stopping at the quadratic formula without checking.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nQuadratics and polynomials reuse linear factor and distribution habits with higher degree.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Quadratic Inequalities” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 9 and -3. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a savings plan for college costs). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. Solve and graph on a number line: 6x + 1 > 43. State the solution in interval notation.\n2. Solve and graph on a number line: 4x + 1 ≥ 29. State the solution in interval notation.\n3. Solve and graph on a number line: 3x + 3 > 12. State the solution in interval notation.\n4. Solve and graph on a number line: 3x + 2 ≥ 26. State the solution in interval notation.\n5. Solve and graph on a number line: 2x + 1 > 15. State the solution in interval notation.\n6. Solve and graph on a number line: 5x + -2 ≥ 18. State the solution in interval notation.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. x > 7; interval (7, ∞).\n2. x ≥ 7; interval [7, ∞).\n3. x > 3; interval (3, ∞).\n4. x ≥ 8; interval [8, ∞).\n5. x > 7; interval (7, ∞).\n6. x ≥ 4; interval [4, ∞).\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Quadratic Inequalities” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 2: Quadratic Functions.\n",
