    content: "# Polynomial Division\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 3 of 10 · Polynomials · Lesson 5*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** divide polynomials with long division; interpret quotient and remainder.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a construction slope measurement that might use **Polynomial Division**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 3 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nPolynomials are finite sums of power terms. Degree and leading coefficient drive end behavior. Today's skill — **Polynomial Division** — builds that habit inside **Polynomials**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nVolume formulas, expanded products, and curve sketching start with clean polynomial algebra. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **degree** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **leading coefficient** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **multiplicity** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a construction slope measurement. Goal aligned to “Polynomial Division.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 3, -2, 3.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Canceling terms instead of factors, or dropping signs when distributing negatives.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Polynomial Division, computed with visible steps using 3 and -2, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Polynomial Division” but change the story to a savings plan for college costs. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Canceling terms instead of factors, or dropping signs when distributing negatives.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nQuadratics and polynomials reuse linear factor and distribution habits with higher degree.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Polynomial Division” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 3 and -2. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a construction slope measurement). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. Add (2x² − 7x + 4) + (x² + 7x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n2. Add (2x² − 10x + 6) + (x² + 8x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n3. Add (2x² − 3x + 6) + (x² + 6x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n4. Add (2x² − 6x + 9) + (x² + 6x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n5. Add (2x² − 9x + 5) + (x² + 7x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n6. Add (2x² − 2x + 5) + (x² + 5x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. 3x² + (0)x + 3; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n2. 3x² + (-2)x + 5; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n3. 3x² + (3)x + 5; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n4. 3x² + (0)x + 8; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n5. 3x² + (-2)x + 4; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n6. 3x² + (3)x + 4; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Polynomial Division” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 3: Polynomials.\n",
