    content: "# Zeros & Multiplicity Intro\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 3 of 10 · Polynomials · Lesson 7*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** relate factors to zeros; sketch multiplicity effects on the graph.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a music festival ticket model that might use **Zeros & Multiplicity Intro**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 3 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nPolynomials are finite sums of power terms. Degree and leading coefficient drive end behavior. Today's skill — **Zeros & Multiplicity Intro** — builds that habit inside **Polynomials**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nVolume formulas, expanded products, and curve sketching start with clean polynomial algebra. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **degree** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **leading coefficient** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **multiplicity** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a music festival ticket model. Goal aligned to “Zeros & Multiplicity Intro.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 10, 0, 4.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Canceling terms instead of factors, or dropping signs when distributing negatives.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Zeros & Multiplicity Intro, computed with visible steps using 10 and 0, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Zeros & Multiplicity Intro” but change the story to a school fundraiser spreadsheet. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Canceling terms instead of factors, or dropping signs when distributing negatives.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nQuadratics and polynomials reuse linear factor and distribution habits with higher degree.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Zeros & Multiplicity Intro” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 10 and 0. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a music festival ticket model). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. Add (2x² − 6x + 5) + (x² + 3x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n2. Add (2x² − 5x + 10) + (x² + 4x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n3. Add (2x² − 8x + 7) + (x² + 3x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n4. Add (2x² − 7x + 6) + (x² + 4x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n5. Add (2x² − 6x + 5) + (x² + 5x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n6. Add (2x² − 2x + 8) + (x² + 3x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. 3x² + (-3)x + 4; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n2. 3x² + (-1)x + 9; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n3. 3x² + (-5)x + 6; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n4. 3x² + (-3)x + 5; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n5. 3x² + (-1)x + 4; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n6. 3x² + (1)x + 7; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Zeros & Multiplicity Intro” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 3: Polynomials.\n",
