    content: "# Polynomial Unit Synthesis\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 3 of 10 · Polynomials · Lesson 8*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** solve a mixed set requiring operations, factoring, and zeros.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a small business break-even table that might use **Polynomial Unit Synthesis**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 3 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nPolynomials are finite sums of power terms. Degree and leading coefficient drive end behavior. Today's skill — **Polynomial Unit Synthesis** — builds that habit inside **Polynomials**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nVolume formulas, expanded products, and curve sketching start with clean polynomial algebra. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **degree** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **leading coefficient** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **multiplicity** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a small business break-even table. Goal aligned to “Polynomial Unit Synthesis.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 11, 6, 9.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Canceling terms instead of factors, or dropping signs when distributing negatives.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Polynomial Unit Synthesis, computed with visible steps using 11 and 6, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Polynomial Unit Synthesis” but change the story to a science lab data set. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Canceling terms instead of factors, or dropping signs when distributing negatives.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nQuadratics and polynomials reuse linear factor and distribution habits with higher degree.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Polynomial Unit Synthesis” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 11 and 6. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a small business break-even table). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. Add (2x² − 4x + 4) + (x² + 0x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n2. Add (2x² − 10x + 4) + (x² + 1x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n3. Add (2x² − 6x + 0) + (x² + -1x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n4. Add (2x² − 7x + 1) + (x² + 3x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n5. Add (2x² − 6x + 0) + (x² + 0x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n6. Add (2x² − 2x + 2) + (x² + -2x − 1). State degree and leading coefficient of the sum.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. 3x² + (-4)x + 3; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n2. 3x² + (-9)x + 3; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n3. 3x² + (-7)x + -1; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n4. 3x² + (-4)x + 0; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n5. 3x² + (-6)x + -1; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n6. 3x² + (-4)x + 1; degree 2; leading coefficient 3.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Polynomial Unit Synthesis” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 3: Polynomials.\n",
