    content: "# Domain of Rational Expressions\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 4 of 10 · Rational Expressions & Equations · Lesson 1*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** state excluded values where denominators are zero.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a construction slope measurement that might use **Domain of Rational Expressions**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 4 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nRational expressions are polynomial ratios. Domain exclusions are part of the answer. Today's skill — **Domain of Rational Expressions** — builds that habit inside **Rational Expressions & Equations**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nRates, densities, and average-cost models naturally produce rational equations. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **excluded value** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **LCD** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **extraneous solution** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a construction slope measurement. Goal aligned to “Domain of Rational Expressions.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 9, -1, 4.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Canceling addends across a fraction bar, or keeping a root that zeros a denominator.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Domain of Rational Expressions, computed with visible steps using 9 and -1, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Domain of Rational Expressions” but change the story to a savings plan for college costs. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Canceling addends across a fraction bar, or keeping a root that zeros a denominator.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nRationals, radicals, and exponentials extend equation-solving with domain and inverse awareness.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Domain of Rational Expressions” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 9 and -1. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a construction slope measurement). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. Simplify (2x²)/(2x) for x ≠ 0. State the simplified expression and excluded value.\n2. Simplify (3x²)/(3x) for x ≠ 0. State the simplified expression and excluded value.\n3. Simplify (4x²)/(4x) for x ≠ 0. State the simplified expression and excluded value.\n4. Simplify (5x²)/(5x) for x ≠ 0. State the simplified expression and excluded value.\n5. Simplify (6x²)/(6x) for x ≠ 0. State the simplified expression and excluded value.\n6. Simplify (2x²)/(2x) for x ≠ 0. State the simplified expression and excluded value.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. x (for x ≠ 0). Excluded: x = 0.\n2. x (for x ≠ 0). Excluded: x = 0.\n3. x (for x ≠ 0). Excluded: x = 0.\n4. x (for x ≠ 0). Excluded: x = 0.\n5. x (for x ≠ 0). Excluded: x = 0.\n6. x (for x ≠ 0). Excluded: x = 0.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Domain of Rational Expressions” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 4: Rational Expressions & Equations.\n",
