    description: "Solve rational equations; check extraneous solutions.",
    objectives: "• Solve rational equations; check extraneous solutions.\n• Show algebraic structure with labeled steps.\n• Check solutions (substitution, estimate, or domain).",
    content: "# Rational Equations\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 4 of 10 · Rational Expressions & Equations · Lesson 6*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** solve rational equations; check extraneous solutions.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a music festival ticket model that might use **Rational Equations**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 4 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nRational expressions are polynomial ratios. Domain exclusions are part of the answer. Today's skill — **Rational Equations** — builds that habit inside **Rational Expressions & Equations**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nRates, densities, and average-cost models naturally produce rational equations. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **excluded value** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **LCD** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **extraneous solution** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a music festival ticket model. Goal aligned to “Rational Equations.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 8, 5, 5.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Canceling addends across a fraction bar, or keeping a root that zeros a denominator.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Rational Equations, computed with visible steps using 8 and 5, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Rational Equations” but change the story to a school fundraiser spreadsheet. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Canceling addends across a fraction bar, or keeping a root that zeros a denominator.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nRationals, radicals, and exponentials extend equation-solving with domain and inverse awareness.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Rational Equations” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 8 and 5. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a music festival ticket model). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. Solve (x + 5)/(x − 8) = 2. Check for extraneous solutions (domain x ≠ 8).\n2. Solve (x + 4)/(x − 7) = 2. Check for extraneous solutions (domain x ≠ 7).\n3. Solve (x + 7)/(x − 4) = 2. Check for extraneous solutions (domain x ≠ 4).\n4. Solve (x + 6)/(x − 9) = 2. Check for extraneous solutions (domain x ≠ 9).\n5. Solve (x + 5)/(x − 8) = 2. Check for extraneous solutions (domain x ≠ 8).\n6. Solve (x + 8)/(x − 5) = 2. Check for extraneous solutions (domain x ≠ 5).\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. x + 5 = 2(x − 8) ⇒ x + 5 = 2x − 16 ⇒ x = 21. Valid if ≠ 8.\n2. x + 4 = 2(x − 7) ⇒ x + 4 = 2x − 14 ⇒ x = 18. Valid if ≠ 7.\n3. x + 7 = 2(x − 4) ⇒ x + 7 = 2x − 8 ⇒ x = 15. Valid if ≠ 4.\n4. x + 6 = 2(x − 9) ⇒ x + 6 = 2x − 18 ⇒ x = 24. Valid if ≠ 9.\n5. x + 5 = 2(x − 8) ⇒ x + 5 = 2x − 16 ⇒ x = 21. Valid if ≠ 8.\n6. x + 8 = 2(x − 5) ⇒ x + 8 = 2x − 10 ⇒ x = 18. Valid if ≠ 5.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Rational Equations” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 4: Rational Expressions & Equations.\n",
