    content: "# Add & Multiply Radicals\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 5 of 10 · Radicals & Rational Exponents · Lesson 3*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** combine like radicals; multiply with distribution.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a robotics build schedule that might use **Add & Multiply Radicals**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 5 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nRadicals and rational exponents are two spellings of the same idea — with domain care. Today's skill — **Add & Multiply Radicals** — builds that habit inside **Radicals & Rational Exponents**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nDistance, geometry, and scaled roots appear constantly in science and design. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **principal root** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **index** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **rationalize** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a robotics build schedule. Goal aligned to “Add & Multiply Radicals.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 6, 11, 7.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Writing √(a²)=a for all real a without absolute value thinking, or skipping the check step.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Add & Multiply Radicals, computed with visible steps using 6 and 11, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Add & Multiply Radicals” but change the story to a track practice pacing plan. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Writing √(a²)=a for all real a without absolute value thinking, or skipping the check step.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nRationals, radicals, and exponentials extend equation-solving with domain and inverse awareness.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Add & Multiply Radicals” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 6 and 11. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a robotics build schedule). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. Rewrite 9x⁴ under a square root as a simplified radical (assume x ≥ 0).\n2. Rewrite 100x⁴ under a square root as a simplified radical (assume x ≥ 0).\n3. Rewrite 36x⁴ under a square root as a simplified radical (assume x ≥ 0).\n4. Rewrite 36x⁴ under a square root as a simplified radical (assume x ≥ 0).\n5. Rewrite 25x⁴ under a square root as a simplified radical (assume x ≥ 0).\n6. Rewrite 4x⁴ under a square root as a simplified radical (assume x ≥ 0).\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. √(9x⁴) = 3x².\n2. √(100x⁴) = 10x².\n3. √(36x⁴) = 6x².\n4. √(36x⁴) = 6x².\n5. √(25x⁴) = 5x².\n6. √(4x⁴) = 2x².\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Add & Multiply Radicals” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 5: Radicals & Rational Exponents.\n",
