    content: "# Radical Equations\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 5 of 10 · Radicals & Rational Exponents · Lesson 5*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** solve radical equations; check for extraneous roots.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a science lab data set that might use **Radical Equations**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 5 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nRadicals and rational exponents are two spellings of the same idea — with domain care. Today's skill — **Radical Equations** — builds that habit inside **Radicals & Rational Exponents**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nDistance, geometry, and scaled roots appear constantly in science and design. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **principal root** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **index** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **rationalize** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a science lab data set. Goal aligned to “Radical Equations.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 10, 7, 5.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Writing √(a²)=a for all real a without absolute value thinking, or skipping the check step.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Radical Equations, computed with visible steps using 10 and 7, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Radical Equations” but change the story to a small business break-even table. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Writing √(a²)=a for all real a without absolute value thinking, or skipping the check step.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nRationals, radicals, and exponentials extend equation-solving with domain and inverse awareness.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Radical Equations” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 10 and 7. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a science lab data set). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. Solve √(x + 9) = 5. Check the solution in the original equation.\n2. Solve √(x + 4) = 7. Check the solution in the original equation.\n3. Solve √(x + 4) = 6. Check the solution in the original equation.\n4. Solve √(x + 6) = 8. Check the solution in the original equation.\n5. Solve √(x + 9) = 5. Check the solution in the original equation.\n6. Solve √(x + 8) = 4. Check the solution in the original equation.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. x + 9 = 25 ⇒ x = 16. Check: √(25) = 5.\n2. x + 4 = 49 ⇒ x = 45. Check: √(49) = 7.\n3. x + 4 = 36 ⇒ x = 32. Check: √(36) = 6.\n4. x + 6 = 64 ⇒ x = 58. Check: √(64) = 8.\n5. x + 9 = 25 ⇒ x = 16. Check: √(25) = 5.\n6. x + 8 = 16 ⇒ x = 8. Check: √(16) = 4.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Radical Equations” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 5: Radicals & Rational Exponents.\n",
