    content: "# Intro to Logarithms\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 6 of 10 · Exponential & Logarithmic Functions · Lesson 3*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** define log_b(a) as the exponent; evaluate simple logs.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a small business break-even table that might use **Intro to Logarithms**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 6 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nExponentials multiply by a constant factor; logs undo that multiplication as exponents. Today's skill — **Intro to Logarithms** — builds that habit inside **Exponential & Logarithmic Functions**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nInterest, population, and half-life are exponential stories — logs solve for time. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **growth factor** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **asymptote** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **logarithm** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a small business break-even table. Goal aligned to “Intro to Logarithms.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 10, 6, 6.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Applying log laws to sums incorrectly, or forgetting arguments of logs must be positive.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Intro to Logarithms, computed with visible steps using 10 and 6, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Intro to Logarithms” but change the story to a science lab data set. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Applying log laws to sums incorrectly, or forgetting arguments of logs must be positive.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nRationals, radicals, and exponentials extend equation-solving with domain and inverse awareness.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Intro to Logarithms” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 10 and 6. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a small business break-even table). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. Evaluate log_2(8) and rewrite log_2(x) = 7 as an exponential equation.\n2. Evaluate log_6(216) and rewrite log_6(x) = 6 as an exponential equation.\n3. Evaluate log_5(125) and rewrite log_5(x) = 2 as an exponential equation.\n4. Evaluate log_2(8) and rewrite log_2(x) = 3 as an exponential equation.\n5. Evaluate log_6(216) and rewrite log_6(x) = 9 as an exponential equation.\n6. Evaluate log_5(125) and rewrite log_5(x) = 5 as an exponential equation.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. log_2(8) = 3; x = 2^7 = 128.\n2. log_6(216) = 3; x = 6^6 = 46656.\n3. log_5(125) = 3; x = 5^2 = 25.\n4. log_2(8) = 3; x = 2^3 = 8.\n5. log_6(216) = 3; x = 6^9 = 10077696.\n6. log_5(125) = 3; x = 5^5 = 3125.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Intro to Logarithms” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 6: Exponential & Logarithmic Functions.\n",
