    content: "# Solving Exponential Equations\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 6 of 10 · Exponential & Logarithmic Functions · Lesson 5*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** solve b^x = c using same-base or logs.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a school fundraiser spreadsheet that might use **Solving Exponential Equations**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 6 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nExponentials multiply by a constant factor; logs undo that multiplication as exponents. Today's skill — **Solving Exponential Equations** — builds that habit inside **Exponential & Logarithmic Functions**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nInterest, population, and half-life are exponential stories — logs solve for time. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **growth factor** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **asymptote** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **logarithm** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a school fundraiser spreadsheet. Goal aligned to “Solving Exponential Equations.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 10, 11, 9.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Applying log laws to sums incorrectly, or forgetting arguments of logs must be positive.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Solving Exponential Equations, computed with visible steps using 10 and 11, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Solving Exponential Equations” but change the story to a music festival ticket model. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Applying log laws to sums incorrectly, or forgetting arguments of logs must be positive.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nRationals, radicals, and exponentials extend equation-solving with domain and inverse awareness.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Solving Exponential Equations” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 10 and 11. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a school fundraiser spreadsheet). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. A balance starts at $400 and grows by factor 5 each year. Write A(t) = A0·b^t and find A(2).\n2. A balance starts at $600 and grows by factor 2 each year. Write A(t) = A0·b^t and find A(2).\n3. A balance starts at $600 and grows by factor 3 each year. Write A(t) = A0·b^t and find A(2).\n4. A balance starts at $300 and grows by factor 3 each year. Write A(t) = A0·b^t and find A(2).\n5. A balance starts at $600 and grows by factor 4 each year. Write A(t) = A0·b^t and find A(2).\n6. A balance starts at $500 and grows by factor 6 each year. Write A(t) = A0·b^t and find A(2).\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. A(t) = 400·5^t; A(2) = 10000.\n2. A(t) = 600·2^t; A(2) = 2400.\n3. A(t) = 600·3^t; A(2) = 5400.\n4. A(t) = 300·3^t; A(2) = 2700.\n5. A(t) = 600·4^t; A(2) = 9600.\n6. A(t) = 500·6^t; A(2) = 18000.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Solving Exponential Equations” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 6: Exponential & Logarithmic Functions.\n",
