    content: "# Solving Logarithmic Equations\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 6 of 10 · Exponential & Logarithmic Functions · Lesson 6*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** solve log equations; check domain (arguments > 0).\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a science lab data set that might use **Solving Logarithmic Equations**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 6 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nExponentials multiply by a constant factor; logs undo that multiplication as exponents. Today's skill — **Solving Logarithmic Equations** — builds that habit inside **Exponential & Logarithmic Functions**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nInterest, population, and half-life are exponential stories — logs solve for time. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **growth factor** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **asymptote** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **logarithm** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a science lab data set. Goal aligned to “Solving Logarithmic Equations.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 7, 8, 10.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Applying log laws to sums incorrectly, or forgetting arguments of logs must be positive.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Solving Logarithmic Equations, computed with visible steps using 7 and 8, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Solving Logarithmic Equations” but change the story to a small business break-even table. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Applying log laws to sums incorrectly, or forgetting arguments of logs must be positive.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nRationals, radicals, and exponentials extend equation-solving with domain and inverse awareness.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Solving Logarithmic Equations” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 7 and 8. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a science lab data set). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. Evaluate log_5(125) and rewrite log_5(x) = 4 as an exponential equation.\n2. Evaluate log_2(8) and rewrite log_2(x) = 6 as an exponential equation.\n3. Evaluate log_3(27) and rewrite log_3(x) = 6 as an exponential equation.\n4. Evaluate log_5(125) and rewrite log_5(x) = 8 as an exponential equation.\n5. Evaluate log_6(216) and rewrite log_6(x) = 4 as an exponential equation.\n6. Evaluate log_3(27) and rewrite log_3(x) = 3 as an exponential equation.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. log_5(125) = 3; x = 5^4 = 625.\n2. log_2(8) = 3; x = 2^6 = 64.\n3. log_3(27) = 3; x = 3^6 = 729.\n4. log_5(125) = 3; x = 5^8 = 390625.\n5. log_6(216) = 3; x = 6^4 = 1296.\n6. log_3(27) = 3; x = 3^3 = 27.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Solving Logarithmic Equations” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 6: Exponential & Logarithmic Functions.\n",
