    content: "# Change of Base & Applications\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 6 of 10 · Exponential & Logarithmic Functions · Lesson 7*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** use change of base; model half-life and compound interest intro.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a savings plan for college costs that might use **Change of Base & Applications**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 6 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nExponentials multiply by a constant factor; logs undo that multiplication as exponents. Today's skill — **Change of Base & Applications** — builds that habit inside **Exponential & Logarithmic Functions**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nInterest, population, and half-life are exponential stories — logs solve for time. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **growth factor** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **asymptote** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **logarithm** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a savings plan for college costs. Goal aligned to “Change of Base & Applications.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 5, -3, 5.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Applying log laws to sums incorrectly, or forgetting arguments of logs must be positive.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Change of Base & Applications, computed with visible steps using 5 and -3, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Change of Base & Applications” but change the story to a construction slope measurement. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Applying log laws to sums incorrectly, or forgetting arguments of logs must be positive.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nRationals, radicals, and exponentials extend equation-solving with domain and inverse awareness.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Change of Base & Applications” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 5 and -3. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a savings plan for college costs). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. A balance starts at $400 and grows by factor 6 each year. Write A(t) = A0·b^t and find A(2).\n2. A balance starts at $400 and grows by factor 4 each year. Write A(t) = A0·b^t and find A(2).\n3. A balance starts at $700 and grows by factor 3 each year. Write A(t) = A0·b^t and find A(2).\n4. A balance starts at $500 and grows by factor 3 each year. Write A(t) = A0·b^t and find A(2).\n5. A balance starts at $400 and grows by factor 2 each year. Write A(t) = A0·b^t and find A(2).\n6. A balance starts at $800 and grows by factor 5 each year. Write A(t) = A0·b^t and find A(2).\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. A(t) = 400·6^t; A(2) = 14400.\n2. A(t) = 400·4^t; A(2) = 6400.\n3. A(t) = 700·3^t; A(2) = 6300.\n4. A(t) = 500·3^t; A(2) = 4500.\n5. A(t) = 400·2^t; A(2) = 1600.\n6. A(t) = 800·5^t; A(2) = 20000.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Change of Base & Applications” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 6: Exponential & Logarithmic Functions.\n",
