    objectives: "• Choose exponential vs log form and solve a multi-step model.\n• Show algebraic structure with labeled steps.\n• Check solutions (substitution, estimate, or domain).",
    content: "# Exp/Log Unit Capstone\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 6 of 10 · Exponential & Logarithmic Functions · Lesson 8*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** choose exponential vs log form and solve a multi-step model.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a music festival ticket model that might use **Exp/Log Unit Capstone**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 6 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nExponentials multiply by a constant factor; logs undo that multiplication as exponents. Today's skill — **Exp/Log Unit Capstone** — builds that habit inside **Exponential & Logarithmic Functions**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nInterest, population, and half-life are exponential stories — logs solve for time. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **growth factor** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **asymptote** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **logarithm** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a music festival ticket model. Goal aligned to “Exp/Log Unit Capstone.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 7, 3, 2.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Applying log laws to sums incorrectly, or forgetting arguments of logs must be positive.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Exp/Log Unit Capstone, computed with visible steps using 7 and 3, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Exp/Log Unit Capstone” but change the story to a school fundraiser spreadsheet. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Applying log laws to sums incorrectly, or forgetting arguments of logs must be positive.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nRationals, radicals, and exponentials extend equation-solving with domain and inverse awareness.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Exp/Log Unit Capstone” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 7 and 3. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a music festival ticket model). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. Evaluate log_6(216) and rewrite log_6(x) = 6 as an exponential equation.\n2. Evaluate log_5(125) and rewrite log_5(x) = 5 as an exponential equation.\n3. Evaluate log_4(64) and rewrite log_4(x) = 8 as an exponential equation.\n4. Evaluate log_3(27) and rewrite log_3(x) = 7 as an exponential equation.\n5. Evaluate log_2(8) and rewrite log_2(x) = 6 as an exponential equation.\n6. Evaluate log_6(216) and rewrite log_6(x) = 2 as an exponential equation.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. log_6(216) = 3; x = 6^6 = 46656.\n2. log_5(125) = 3; x = 5^5 = 3125.\n3. log_4(64) = 3; x = 4^8 = 65536.\n4. log_3(27) = 3; x = 3^7 = 2187.\n5. log_2(8) = 3; x = 2^6 = 64.\n6. log_6(216) = 3; x = 6^2 = 36.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Exp/Log Unit Capstone” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 6: Exponential & Logarithmic Functions.\n",
