    objectives: "• Reduce angles to acute reference angles in standard position.\n• Show algebraic structure with labeled steps.\n• Check solutions (substitution, estimate, or domain).",
    content: "# Reference Angles\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 8 of 10 · Trigonometry Foundations · Lesson 6*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** reduce angles to acute reference angles in standard position.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a robotics build schedule that might use **Reference Angles**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 8 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nTrig ratios compare sides in right triangles; the unit circle extends those ratios to angles. Today's skill — **Reference Angles** — builds that habit inside **Trigonometry Foundations**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nSurveying, ramps, navigation, and periodic motion start with right-triangle trig. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **opposite** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **adjacent** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **hypotenuse** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a robotics build schedule. Goal aligned to “Reference Angles.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 9, -1, 5.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Swapping opposite/adjacent, or using degrees in a calculator set to radians (or vice versa).\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Reference Angles, computed with visible steps using 9 and -1, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Reference Angles” but change the story to a track practice pacing plan. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Swapping opposite/adjacent, or using degrees in a calculator set to radians (or vice versa).\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nSequences, trig, and stats connect algebraic fluency to modeling and data claims.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Reference Angles” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 9 and -1. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a robotics build schedule). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. In a right triangle, opposite = 12, adjacent = 16. Find sin, cos, and tan of the acute angle opposite the 12 side (exact ratios).\n2. In a right triangle, opposite = 12, adjacent = 16. Find sin, cos, and tan of the acute angle opposite the 12 side (exact ratios).\n3. In a right triangle, opposite = 21, adjacent = 28. Find sin, cos, and tan of the acute angle opposite the 21 side (exact ratios).\n4. In a right triangle, opposite = 21, adjacent = 28. Find sin, cos, and tan of the acute angle opposite the 21 side (exact ratios).\n5. In a right triangle, opposite = 18, adjacent = 24. Find sin, cos, and tan of the acute angle opposite the 18 side (exact ratios).\n6. In a right triangle, opposite = 9, adjacent = 12. Find sin, cos, and tan of the acute angle opposite the 9 side (exact ratios).\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. sin = 12/20 = 3/5; cos = 4/5; tan = 3/4 (hypotenuse 20 by 3-4-5).\n2. sin = 12/20 = 3/5; cos = 4/5; tan = 3/4 (hypotenuse 20 by 3-4-5).\n3. sin = 21/35 = 3/5; cos = 4/5; tan = 3/4 (hypotenuse 35 by 3-4-5).\n4. sin = 21/35 = 3/5; cos = 4/5; tan = 3/4 (hypotenuse 35 by 3-4-5).\n5. sin = 18/30 = 3/5; cos = 4/5; tan = 3/4 (hypotenuse 30 by 3-4-5).\n6. sin = 9/15 = 3/5; cos = 4/5; tan = 3/4 (hypotenuse 15 by 3-4-5).\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Reference Angles” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 8: Trigonometry Foundations.\n",
