    objectives: "• Distinguish independent vs dependent; compute AND/OR carefully.\n• Show algebraic structure with labeled steps.\n• Check solutions (substitution, estimate, or domain).",
    content: "# Compound Events\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 9 of 10 · Probability & Statistics · Lesson 3*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** distinguish independent vs dependent; compute AND/OR carefully.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a robotics build schedule that might use **Compound Events**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 9 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nProbability quantifies uncertainty; statistics summarizes data. Both demand careful definitions. Today's skill — **Compound Events** — builds that habit inside **Probability & Statistics**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nCollege-ready citizens read polls, sports stats, and risk claims without being fooled. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **sample space** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **independent** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **conditional** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a robotics build schedule. Goal aligned to “Compound Events.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 3, 7, 6.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Adding probabilities that are not mutually exclusive without inclusion, or confusing mean with median.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Compound Events, computed with visible steps using 3 and 7, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Compound Events” but change the story to a track practice pacing plan. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Adding probabilities that are not mutually exclusive without inclusion, or confusing mean with median.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nSequences, trig, and stats connect algebraic fluency to modeling and data claims.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Compound Events” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 3 and 7. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a robotics build schedule). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. P(A) = 8/12, P(B) = 4/12, independent. Find P(A and B) as a fraction in lowest terms if possible.\n2. P(A) = 8/12, P(B) = 4/12, independent. Find P(A and B) as a fraction in lowest terms if possible.\n3. P(A) = 4/4, P(B) = 0/4, independent. Find P(A and B) as a fraction in lowest terms if possible.\n4. P(A) = 4/4, P(B) = 0/4, independent. Find P(A and B) as a fraction in lowest terms if possible.\n5. P(A) = 10/10, P(B) = 0/10, independent. Find P(A and B) as a fraction in lowest terms if possible.\n6. P(A) = 7/10, P(B) = 3/10, independent. Find P(A and B) as a fraction in lowest terms if possible.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. P(A∩B) = (8/12)·(4/12) = 32/144.\n2. P(A∩B) = (8/12)·(4/12) = 32/144.\n3. P(A∩B) = (4/4)·(0/4) = 0/16.\n4. P(A∩B) = (4/4)·(0/4) = 0/16.\n5. P(A∩B) = (10/10)·(0/10) = 0/100.\n6. P(A∩B) = (7/10)·(3/10) = 21/100.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Compound Events” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 9: Probability & Statistics.\n",
