    id: "ppg10ma196f11330a06a815805",
    title: "Function Families Overview",
    description: "Compare linear, quadratic, exponential, and rational shapes.",
    objectives: "• Compare linear, quadratic, exponential, and rational shapes.\n• Show algebraic structure with labeled steps.\n• Check solutions (substitution, estimate, or domain).",
    content: "# Function Families Overview\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 10 of 10 · Functions, Modeling & Capstone · Lesson 1*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** compare linear, quadratic, exponential, and rational shapes.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a track practice pacing plan that might use **Function Families Overview**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 10 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nModeling means choosing a function family that matches the story — then checking residual sense. Today's skill — **Function Families Overview** — builds that habit inside **Functions, Modeling & Capstone**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nACT/SAT and real work reward flexible function sense more than isolated tricks. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **domain** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **inverse** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **piecewise** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a track practice pacing plan. Goal aligned to “Function Families Overview.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 4, 2, 1.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Forcing a linear fit on clearly curved data, or claiming causation from correlation.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Function Families Overview, computed with visible steps using 4 and 2, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Function Families Overview” but change the story to a robotics build schedule. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Forcing a linear fit on clearly curved data, or claiming causation from correlation.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nSequences, trig, and stats connect algebraic fluency to modeling and data claims.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Function Families Overview” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 4 and 2. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a track practice pacing plan). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. A quantity starts at 90 and changes by +3 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n2. A quantity starts at 50 and changes by ×4 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n3. A quantity starts at 50 and changes by +5 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n4. A quantity starts at 60 and changes by ×3 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n5. A quantity starts at 90 and changes by +4 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n6. A quantity starts at 20 and changes by ×5 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. a1=90; a_{n+1}=a_n+3. Terms: 90, 93, 96.\n2. a1=50; a_{n+1}=4·a_n. Terms: 50, 200, 800.\n3. a1=50; a_{n+1}=a_n+5. Terms: 50, 55, 60.\n4. a1=60; a_{n+1}=3·a_n. Terms: 60, 180, 540.\n5. a1=90; a_{n+1}=a_n+4. Terms: 90, 94, 98.\n6. a1=20; a_{n+1}=5·a_n. Terms: 20, 100, 500.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Function Families Overview” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 10: Functions, Modeling & Capstone.\n",
