    objectives: "• Find domains; invert simple functions and verify with composition.\n• Show algebraic structure with labeled steps.\n• Check solutions (substitution, estimate, or domain).",
    content: "# Domain, Range & Inverses\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 10 of 10 · Functions, Modeling & Capstone · Lesson 2*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** find domains; invert simple functions and verify with composition.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a music festival ticket model that might use **Domain, Range & Inverses**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 10 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nModeling means choosing a function family that matches the story — then checking residual sense. Today's skill — **Domain, Range & Inverses** — builds that habit inside **Functions, Modeling & Capstone**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nACT/SAT and real work reward flexible function sense more than isolated tricks. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **domain** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **inverse** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **piecewise** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a music festival ticket model. Goal aligned to “Domain, Range & Inverses.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 7, 10, 6.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Forcing a linear fit on clearly curved data, or claiming causation from correlation.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Domain, Range & Inverses, computed with visible steps using 7 and 10, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Domain, Range & Inverses” but change the story to a school fundraiser spreadsheet. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Forcing a linear fit on clearly curved data, or claiming causation from correlation.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nSequences, trig, and stats connect algebraic fluency to modeling and data claims.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Domain, Range & Inverses” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 7 and 10. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a music festival ticket model). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. A quantity starts at 90 and changes by +6 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n2. A quantity starts at 80 and changes by ×5 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n3. A quantity starts at 40 and changes by +4 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n4. A quantity starts at 30 and changes by ×3 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n5. A quantity starts at 90 and changes by +2 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n6. A quantity starts at 50 and changes by ×6 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. a1=90; a_{n+1}=a_n+6. Terms: 90, 96, 102.\n2. a1=80; a_{n+1}=5·a_n. Terms: 80, 400, 2000.\n3. a1=40; a_{n+1}=a_n+4. Terms: 40, 44, 48.\n4. a1=30; a_{n+1}=3·a_n. Terms: 30, 90, 270.\n5. a1=90; a_{n+1}=a_n+2. Terms: 90, 92, 94.\n6. a1=50; a_{n+1}=6·a_n. Terms: 50, 300, 1800.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Domain, Range & Inverses” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 10: Functions, Modeling & Capstone.\n",
