    objectives: "• Evaluate and graph piecewise rules; check boundary points.\n• Show algebraic structure with labeled steps.\n• Check solutions (substitution, estimate, or domain).",
    content: "# Piecewise Functions\n\n*Grade 10 Algebra & Beyond · Unit 10 of 10 · Functions, Modeling & Capstone · Lesson 3*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** evaluate and graph piecewise rules; check boundary points.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students show algebraic structure, not only final answers. Prefer labeled steps and a check.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout a calculator first, estimate a reasonable answer for a problem about a construction slope measurement that might use **Piecewise Functions**. Jot one sentence explaining your estimate.\n\nShare: Which idea from earlier in Unit 10 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nModeling means choosing a function family that matches the story — then checking residual sense. Today's skill — **Piecewise Functions** — builds that habit inside **Functions, Modeling & Capstone**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nACT/SAT and real work reward flexible function sense more than isolated tricks. At Prosper Prep we treat careful algebra as a college-ready habit: define variables, show structure, and verify.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **domain** — define it in your own words before computing.\n- **inverse** — connect it to a representation (table, graph, or equation).\n- **piecewise** — use it when you explain a check or a restriction.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nContext: a construction slope measurement. Goal aligned to “Piecewise Functions.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate.** What are we finding, and in what units or form?\n**Step 2 — Structure.** Identify the algebraic form (linear, quadratic, rational, exponential, etc.). Sample anchors: 6, 0, 5.\n**Step 3 — Compute.** Carry each step with reasons (distribute, combine, isolate, factor, or apply a formula).\n**Step 4 — Check.** Substitute back, use an inverse operation, or compare to an estimate. Watch for: Forcing a linear fit on clearly curved data, or claiming causation from correlation.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined the variable, named the structure for Piecewise Functions, computed with visible steps using 6 and 0, then checked by substitution. The result matched my estimate from the warm-up closely enough to trust.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nKeep the structure of “Piecewise Functions” but change the story to a savings plan for college costs. Write a one-sentence goal, show the algebra, and conclude with units.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Forcing a linear fit on clearly curved data, or claiming causation from correlation.\n- **Also watch for:** dropping negative signs; skipping domain restrictions; reporting an unsimplified expression as “done.”\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step, then redo only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nSequences, trig, and stats connect algebraic fluency to modeling and data claims.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Restate the goal of “Piecewise Functions” in one sentence, then solve a mini-version using numbers 6 and 0. Show every step.\n   - *Check:* Structure named; arithmetic checked; answer labeled.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same structure, new context (a construction slope measurement). Explain why your representation matches the situation.\n   - *Check:* Units and domain notes appear when needed.\n\n3. **Error hunt —** A fictional student skipped the check and kept an extraneous root / wrong sign. What two questions would you ask before accepting the work?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Try first; then use the answer key.\n\n1. A quantity starts at 60 and changes by +6 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n2. A quantity starts at 90 and changes by ×2 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n3. A quantity starts at 20 and changes by +3 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n4. A quantity starts at 50 and changes by ×4 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n5. A quantity starts at 80 and changes by +5 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n6. A quantity starts at 80 and changes by ×6 each step. Write a recursive rule for the first three terms.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check only after you attempt each item.*\n\n1. a1=60; a_{n+1}=a_n+6. Terms: 60, 66, 72.\n2. a1=90; a_{n+1}=2·a_n. Terms: 90, 180, 360.\n3. a1=20; a_{n+1}=a_n+3. Terms: 20, 23, 26.\n4. a1=50; a_{n+1}=4·a_n. Terms: 50, 200, 800.\n5. a1=80; a_{n+1}=a_n+5. Terms: 80, 85, 90.\n6. a1=80; a_{n+1}=6·a_n. Terms: 80, 480, 2880.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Piecewise Functions” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Write one common mistake for this lesson and how you would repair it.\n3. Create a 2-step practice item for a classmate on this skill (you do not need to solve it here).\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 algebra shows **structure + check**. Tomorrow’s lesson continues Unit 10: Functions, Modeling & Capstone.\n",
